\fs22
\b SOUL Project File Guide\b0\par
Secure Orbital Uplink Layer quick file walkthrough. This is the short-but-deep version so you can read it fast and still know where the real stuff lives.\par
\par
\b What this guide covers\b0\par
This guide covers project-owned files and folders. I grouped third-party/vendor code like \f1 external/glm \f0 and \f1 external/sqlite \f0 together instead of pretending you want a 400-file side quest.\par
\par
\b Whole project in one sentence\b0\par
Sender builds telemetry -> FrameCodec wraps it -> receiver accepts it -> SQLite stores it -> Qt UI reads it -> OrbitView visualizes it -> scenario buttons and repair commands push back into the sim. Nice little loop fr fr.\par
\par
\b Top level files\b0\par
\b README.md\b0 : Human overview, feature list, run steps, and demo media entry point.\par
\b CMakeLists.txt\b0 : Build map for all targets: receiver, sender, UI, SQLite static lib, and Boost JSON shim.\par
\b scenario_state.txt\b0 : Tiny shared state file used by the UI and sender so Solar Storm mode can sync without building a whole command server.\par
\par
\b Common/\b0\par
\b Common/TelemetryFrame.hpp\b0 : Defines the core telemetry packet fields used by the whole project: satellite id, sequence, timestamp, battery, and temperature.\par
\b Common/TelemetryFrame.cpp\b0 : Serializes telemetry to JSON and safely validates/parses it back using Boost.JSON.\par
\b Common/FrameCodec.hpp\b0 : Declares the length-prefixed framing helpers used on the TCP socket.\par
\b Common/FrameCodec.cpp\b0 : Adds a 4-byte big-endian payload size header and reads it back safely on the receive side.\par
\b Common/ScenarioState.hpp\b0 : Shared scenario enum and helpers for Normal, Solar Storm, and Recovery. Also reads/writes the small shared scenario text file.\par
\b Common/BoostJson.cpp\b0 : One-file Boost.JSON implementation shim so the project does not need a prebuilt Boost.JSON binary.\par
\par
\b ReceiverServer/\b0\par
\b ReceiverServer/main.cpp\b0 : Tiny bootstrap that starts the backend listener on port 5000.\par
\b ReceiverServer/SatelliteSim.hpp\b0 : Declares the TCP receiver class, accept loop, per-client handler, and in-memory latest telemetry map.\par
\b ReceiverServer/SatelliteSim.cpp\b0 : Accepts sender connections, decodes frames, validates telemetry, stores rows in SQLite, and replies with ACK frames.\par
\par
\b Database/\b0\par
\b Database/Database.hpp\b0 : Static database helper for init, table creation, inserts, and shutdown.\par
\b Database/Database.cpp\b0 : Opens \f1 Soul.db\f0 , creates \f1 Telemetry\f0 and \f1 ControlCommands\f0 , and writes telemetry rows from the receiver loop.\par
\par
\b SatelliteSender/\b0\par
\b SatelliteSender/main.cpp\b0 : Tiny sender bootstrap that calls \f1 TelemetryHub::runClient()\f0 .\par
\b SatelliteSender/TelemetryHub.hpp\b0 : Declares the sender loop and client socket bootstrap.\par
\b SatelliteSender/TelemetryHub.cpp\b0 : Owns the three simulated satellites, polls repair commands from SQLite, reads scenario state, sends framed telemetry, and waits for ACK after each frame.\par
\par
\b simulation/\b0\par
\b simulation/SimulationState.hpp\b0 : Declares per-satellite behavior state like battery drain, temp drift, signal loss, and repair cooldown.\par
\b simulation/SimulationState.cpp\b0 : Generates believable telemetry per tick, applies storm damage/recovery logic, and can temporarily improve a satellite when a repair command lands.\par
\par
\b ui/\b0\par
\b ui/main.cpp\b0 : Qt app bootstrap.\par
\b ui/mainwindow.ui\b0 : Base widget layout made in Qt Designer. Holds the two top status panels, the satellite table, telemetry table, and the promoted OrbitView widget.\par
\b ui/OperatorActionBox.hpp\b0 : Small helper class that owns the hand-built operator controls like storm/reset/repair and the warning/message labels.\par
\b ui/OperatorActionBox.cpp\b0 : Builds and styles those extra buttons/labels so \f1 mainwindow.cpp \f0 stays lighter.\par
\b ui/mainwindow.h\b0 : Main dashboard class declaration, telemetry refresh helpers, selection state, and DB lookup helpers.\par
\b ui/mainwindow.cpp\b0 : The operator brain. Reads SQLite, fills both tables, updates detail labels, forwards selection to OrbitView, triggers scenarios, and inserts repair commands.\par
\par
\b OrbitalRendering/\b0\par
\b OrbitalRendering/Camera.hpp\b0 : Orbit camera interface for yaw, pitch, zoom, reset, and matrix generation.\par
\b OrbitalRendering/Camera.cpp\b0 : Converts camera orbit values into view/projection matrices used by OpenGL.\par
\b OrbitalRendering/Shader/ShaderSource.hpp\b0 : Stores shader source strings for Earth, satellites, orbit lines, glow points, stars, and storm effects.\par
\b OrbitalRendering/render/MeshData.hpp\b0 : Small packed CPU-side mesh container: interleaved vertices + indices.\par
\b OrbitalRendering/render/ObjLoader.hpp\b0 : Declares the OBJ-to-MeshData loader function.\par
\b OrbitalRendering/render/ObjLoader.cpp\b0 : Parses OBJ positions, UVs, normals, face tokens, and packs them into the exact 8-float vertex layout the renderer expects.\par
\b OrbitalRendering/render/TexturedMesh.hpp\b0 : Reusable mesh wrapper for VAO/VBO/EBO/texture ownership.\par
\b OrbitalRendering/render/TexturedMesh.cpp\b0 : Loads OBJ data, uploads it to GL, creates the texture, and draws the mesh with the current shader program.\par
\b OrbitalRendering/main/SatelliteVisual.hpp\b0 : Orbit math-only helper for satellite radius, angle, speed, tilt, phase, and scale.\par
\b OrbitalRendering/main/SatelliteVisual.cpp\b0 : Updates orbit motion over time and builds the model matrix for each satellite.\par
\b OrbitalRendering/main/orbitview.hpp\b0 : Declares the main OpenGL widget and all scene-side state for Earth, satellites, stars, storm visuals, and repair tinting.\par
\b OrbitalRendering/main/orbitview.cpp\b0 : The visual heart of the demo. Draws the star backdrop, Earth, orbit trails, satellites, glow markers, storm pulse/wave, and repair recovery cues while keeping camera movement alive.\par
\par
\b scratchTests/\b0\par
\b scratchTests/TestFrameCodec.cpp\b0 : Tiny manual sanity test for JSON round-trip and the frame header math.\par
\par
\b docs/\b0\par
\b docs/01_requirement.md\b0 : Project requirement / scope notes.\par
\b docs/02_protocol.md\b0 : Framed TCP payload + ACK flow summary.\par
\b docs/03_architecture.md\b0 : System architecture write-up.\par
\b docs/SOUL.gif, SOUL_preview.gif, SOUL.mp4\b0 : Demo media.\par
\b docs/KanbanBoard.png, TimeLineview.png\b0 : Planning / project tracking visuals.\par
\par
\b Vendor folders\b0\par
\b external/sqlite/\b0 : Bundled SQLite source so the project can build the DB layer directly.\par
\b external/glm/\b0 : Math library used mainly by the OBJ loader and rendering helpers.\par
\par
\b Code snippets worth caring about\b0\par
\par
\b 1. Core telemetry packet\b0\par
{\f1
struct TelemetryFrame \{\par
    std::string sat_id;\par
    uint64_t sequence;\par
    uint64_t timestamp_ms;\par
    float battery;\par
    float temp_c;\par
\};\par
}\f0
Why it matters: this is the one shape that touches sender, receiver, DB, and UI. If this changes, basically everything changes.\par
\par
\b 2. Frame codec idea\b0\par
{\f1
buffer.push_back((json_size >> 24) \& 0xFF);\par
buffer.push_back((json_size >> 16) \& 0xFF);\par
buffer.push_back((json_size >> 8) \& 0xFF);\par
buffer.push_back(json_size \& 0xFF);\par
}\f0
Why it matters: TCP is a byte stream, not a message queue, so this 4-byte header is what tells the receiver where one telemetry message ends and the next one begins.\par
\par
\b 3. Simulation personality hook\b0\par
{\f1
if (mode == SatelliteMode::PowerDrain \&\& stormIsActive)\par
\{\par
    batteryDropThisTick += 0.80f;\par
\}\par
}\f0
Why it matters: this is the main trick that makes SAT\_1, SAT\_2, and SAT\_3 feel different instead of being clone spam.\par
\par
\b 4. Repair command handoff\b0\par
{\f1
INSERT INTO ControlCommands (satellite_name, command, created_ms, processed)\par
VALUES (?, 'REPAIR', ?, 0)\par
}\f0
Why it matters: this is the small bridge that lets the operator dashboard push an action back into the running sim without rewriting the networking layer.\par
\par
\b 5. OrbitView selection rule\b0\par
{\f1
if (hasSelection \&\& !isSelected)\par
\{\par
    continue;\par
\}\par
}\f0
Why it matters: this is the reason the scene can show all satellites by default, then isolate just one when the operator clicks its row.\par
\par
\b Best files to read first if you only have 15 minutes\b0\par
1. \f1 README.md\f0\par
2. \f1 Common/TelemetryFrame.hpp\f0 + \f1 Common/FrameCodec.cpp\f0\par
3. \f1 SatelliteSender/TelemetryHub.cpp\f0\par
4. \f1 ReceiverServer/SatelliteSim.cpp\f0\par
5. \f1 ui/mainwindow.cpp\f0\par
6. \f1 OrbitalRendering/main/orbitview.cpp\f0\par
\par
\b Tiny reading order if you want the real project flow\b0\par
Sender main -> TelemetryHub -> SimulationState -> FrameCodec -> SatelliteSim -> Database -> mainwindow -> OrbitView.\par
\par
End of guide. Short enough to survive, deep enough to actually help. Nice.\par
